written("w2_zero_velocity_zero_acceleration_claim", 2, 5, "2c",
        stem="A student says: 'If an object's velocity is zero, its acceleration must also be zero.' Evaluate this claim using the example of a ball thrown upwards.",
        points=[P("the claim is wrong", ["wrong", "incorrect", "not true", "false", "disagree", "not necessarily"]),
                P("at the top of its flight the ball's velocity is momentarily zero", ["top", "highest point", "momentarily zero", "velocity is zero", "stops for an instant"]),
                P("but gravity still acts, so its acceleration is 10 m/s² downwards; acceleration is the RATE OF CHANGE of velocity, not the velocity itself", ["still 10", "acceleration is 10", "gravity still acts", "weight still acts", "rate of change", "velocity is changing", "about to move down", "not zero"])],
        min_points=2,
        model_answer="The claim is wrong. A ball thrown upwards has zero velocity for an instant at the top of its flight, yet gravity still acts on it, so its acceleration is still 10 m/s² downwards. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, not the velocity itself: the velocity is zero only momentarily and is changing from upwards to downwards.",
        hints=["Think of the very top of the throw. Is the ball moving? Is anything still pulling on it?",
               "Acceleration is about how velocity CHANGES, not about its current value.",
               "State whether the claim holds and explain with the ball at the top of its flight."],
        keywords="claim wrong; velocity zero at top; acceleration still 10 m/s² (rate of change of velocity)",
        sample_pass=["Wrong: at the top the ball's velocity is zero but gravity still accelerates it at 10 m/s² downwards", "incorrect, acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so a momentarily stationary ball can still be accelerating"],
        sample_fail=["Correct, if it is not moving it cannot accelerate", "The ball has no acceleration at the top"])

